Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

FullHD display mode policy checks

Run: bash review-tests/video-mode-policy/run.sh

The test runner extracts the dimension-pass conditions directly from
bios.zero and the preference-selection block directly from loader.zero.
It verifies that loader32.zero contains the identical preference block.
Those current Zero source fragments are compiled with the own compiler and
executed in a hosted assertion program; Python/shell only assemble the test.

Forty assertions cover BIOS FullHD/800x600/compact passes and their dimension
boundaries, plus GOP candidate order, FullHD preferred over earlier/later
fallbacks, 800x600 over640x480 over other compatible modes, invalid-mode
rejection, stable first-choice behavior and an all-invalid candidate list.

This is a source-policy test. It does not emulate QueryMode/SetMode firmware,
framebuffer MMIO, RGB masks, pitch or physical-memory mappings. The integrated
BIOS/UEFI virtual-machine suite separately verifies actual mode selection,
rendered dimensions and lower-resolution fallback.
